{% comment %}
=====================================================================
 A report with charts — one line each, drawn twice
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 Set the template's OUTPUT to "One document for the whole set".

 `{% chart %}` emits NO markup of its own. What it does is record what
 should be drawn, and the output decides how:

   in the HTML preview  a <canvas> plus a JSON data block, drawn by the
                        bundled Chart.js — no CDN, no network
   in the PDF           an inline <svg> computed on the server: vector,
                        selectable, clickable, and no JavaScript at all

 Both are laid out by the same calculation, so the axis, the ticks, the
 colours and the plot rectangle are identical in the two documents.

 WHAT YOU DO NOT WRITE, and could not get right by hand: a canvas size, a
 readiness handshake so the PDF engine knows when to snapshot, an escape
 filter on every data value, or a `responsive: false` that exists because
 of one engine's 2011 browser.

 Nothing here disables a legend. A chart whose meaning rests on colour
 alone fails the linter beside this editor — on a pie that is an error,
 because a greyscale print of four unlabelled slices carries no findings
 at all.
=====================================================================
{% endcomment %}

{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: status, drill: true, assign_to: by_status %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: assignee, sort: count, limit: 10, drill: true, assign_to: by_assignee %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, period: month, periods: 6, assign_to: per_month %}

<h1>Issue report — {{ project.name }}</h1>
<p class="rrd-muted">{{ by_status.total }} issues in scope</p>

<div class="rrd-card">
  {% chart id: status, from: by_status, title: "Issues by status", y_title: "Issues" %}
</div>

<div class="rrd-card">
  {% comment %} Horizontal, because names are long: the layout reserves the label width on
     the correct side rather than clipping it. {% endcomment %}
  {% chart id: assignee, from: by_assignee, orientation: horizontal,
     title: "Open issues by assignee" %}
</div>

<div class="rrd-card">
  {% comment %} `y:` names which of the parallel arrays to draw. The aggregation also
     returns `open_now`, and putting a single number on this axis would be a chart nobody
     asked for. {% endcomment %}
  {% chart id: trend, from: per_month, type: line, y: "created,closed",
     title: "Created and closed per month", x_title: "Month" %}
</div>

<div class="rrd-card">
  <h2>The numbers behind the charts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Status</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Issues</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for bucket in by_status.buckets %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ bucket.label }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ bucket.count }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% comment %} A chart and its table together: a reader who cannot see the chart — a
     screen reader, a greyscale print, an engine that draws nothing — still gets the
     findings. The SVG carries a <title> and a <desc> with the numbers in them for the
     same reason. {% endcomment %}
</div>
